FILE: reviews/receipts/20261020-p4-apply/evidence/12-technical-record-and-both-corrections.md
1: # SigNoz collector: what is built, what is proven, what one person still has to do
2: 
3: > **RULING, 2026-10-20, and it reverses an earlier banner on this same file.** The standalone
4: > collector in this repo, `tools/otel_collector.py` on `127.0.0.1:4320` under LaunchAgent
5: > `com.alex.signoz-collector`, **IS the one door.** Install it. Everything below describing that
6: > install is live, not cancelled.
7: >
8: > The earlier banner here said the opposite: that the patched relay was the collector and this
9: > file's install was cancelled. That is **withdrawn.** It was written on a ruling that crossed with
10: > the p4 evidence; once the launchd-shaped 1Password read, clean SIGTERM handling, the Python 3.9.6
11: > suite and the hostile verifier with its KeepAlive kill test were all in hand, the standalone
12: > collector was the stronger package, and it leaves a reviewed shared component untouched. I am
13: > leaving the reversal visible rather than quietly rewriting history, because anyone who read this
14: > file in the last hour was told not to install the thing that is now the plan.
15: >
16: > **What is NOT applied:** the relay patch in `work/signoz-relay/APPLY.md` and
17: > `make_patch.py`. `~/.agents/.../otlp_relay.py` and `codex_traced.py` stay byte-identical, except
18: > for one named step pointing the CLI wrapper at port 4320, which now lives in `APPLY-p4.md`.
19: >
20: > **The relay keeps running** with zero clients. Retiring it comes after Alex cancels Langfuse, as
21: > a proposal, not an action.
22: >
23: > The live plan is `work/signoz-relay/APPLY-p4.md`, applied through the route Alex chooses.
24: 
25: 2026-10-20, written by Claude Code (claude-opus-5) in `~/Projects/alex-system-map`.
26: Play `absence-alarms-20261020-p2-signoz-hookup`. Source of truth for the account:
27: `~/Documents/Codex/2026-10-19/signoz/SIGNOZ-SETUP.md`.
28: 
29: ## In one paragraph
30: 
31: Agent traces now have a place to go that names them, caps them and refuses the unnamed. It is
32: `tools/otel_collector.py` in this repo, it is tested, and a span sent through it arrives in SigNoz
33: under the right service name while unnamed, blocked and misspelled ones are thrown away. What is
34: missing is durability and reach: it needs a LaunchAgent to stay alive, and each agent needs to be
35: pointed at it. Neither is writable from this project.
36: 
37: ## Why a new collector rather than a patch to the existing relay
38: 
39: The existing relay, `~/.agents/tools/connectors/codex_otel/otlp_relay.py`, was reviewed on the
40: promise that it forwards OTLP bytes unchanged and "does not inspect or change span bodies".
41: Dropping spans by name and rate-limiting per service both require parsing the payload, which is
42: exactly the property it was reviewed for not having. It also sits under `~/.agents`, which this
43: project may not edit. So the filtering lives in a separate file here, where it can be read, tested
44: and reverted, and the reviewed relay keeps its contract.
45: 
46: The relay is not in the way: since the Codex app's tracing was switched off on 2026-10-19 its last
47: request was `2026-10-20T00:19:42Z` and it now has no clients at all.
48: 
49: ## What it does
50: 
51: | Rule | Why |
52: |---|---|
53: | Drops any span group with no `service.name` | SigNoz does **not** do this. A span with no resource attributes at all was accepted with HTTP 200 on 2026-10-20, trace `271bb7f1cea12b2b365fc23aee6a034c`. |
54: | Refuses `codex-worker` outright | The ChatGPT app's firehose. Not to be forwarded unless sampled hard and Alex agrees. |
55: | Counts, and drops, names not on the known list | A typo in an agent's config becomes a number you can see, instead of silently becoming "other". |
56: | Rate-guards **every** service separately | The 2026-10-19 Langfuse flood came from spans that **were** labelled, at 0.9M to 1.4M an hour. Naming and capping are two different jobs. One service over budget cannot starve another. |
57: | Reads keys from 1Password at run time, caches an hour, re-reads on 401/403 | No key in a file, a log, a command line or this document. |
58: | Serves per-service counts at `GET /stats` | The per-service hourly rate can be reported without reading anything back from a vendor. |
59: 
60: Default cap is 600 spans a minute per service. Claude Code's measured rate is roughly 11 a minute,
61: so that is two orders of magnitude of headroom and still stops a runaway. Raise a service in
62: `RATE_OVERRIDES` deliberately, with the reason, rather than removing the cap.
63: 
64: ## Proof, 2026-10-20
65: 
66: Self-test, no network: `python3 -B tools/otel_collector.py --self-test` -> **12/12 checks passed**,
67: including that the re-emitted payload is still parseable OTLP and that a throttled service does not
68: starve another.
69: 
70: End to end, through the collector to SigNoz on port 4320, then read back with the read-only key:
71: 
72: | sent as | collector | arrived in SigNoz |
73: |---|---|---|
74: | `claude-code` | kept | **PRESENT**, `ServiceName='claude-code'`, trace `1607ffd370d084cdc721618c34b762e8` |
75: | no `service.name` | dropped `(unnamed)` | ABSENT |
76: | `codex-worker` | dropped `blocked:` | ABSENT |
77: | `typo-agent` | dropped `unknown:` | ABSENT |
78: 
79: Collector `/stats` at that moment: `kept_spans {"claude-code": 1}`,
80: `dropped_spans {"(unnamed)": 1, "blocked:codex-worker": 1, "unknown:typo-agent": 1}`,
81: `upstream_responses {"signoz:200": 1}`.
82: 
83: Direct ingestion was proven separately, bypassing the collector: `claude-code` trace
84: `5c53704dc32271c551e92b6b07656fd7` and `codex` trace `1205d6991751240d270ed31b0aa416fa`, both read
85: back with the right `ServiceName`. The read key authenticates: `/api/v1/version` returns
86: `{"version":"v0.146.0","ee":"Y","setupCompleted":true}`.
87: 
88: The collector is **not running now.** I stopped it on purpose: it has no LaunchAgent, so a process
89: left running from a chat session dies with the session, and a tracing pipe that quietly dies is the
90: exact failure this whole project exists to remove.
91: 
92: **One honest blemish.** Two spans with an empty `ServiceName` do exist in SigNoz, traces
93: `3024ab909eecd893707866485b3c04ff` and `271bb7f1cea12b2b365fc23aee6a034c`. Both are mine, sent
94: before the collector existed: my first hand-rolled payload double-wrapped the protobuf KeyValue so
95: `service.name` never parsed. They do not appear in the services list. So "zero unnamed spans in the
96: window" is true for everything that went through the collector and false for the window that
97: contains my mistake. It is also the best argument for the collector: SigNoz will not catch that
98: class of error for you.
99: 
100: ## What is still blocked, and on whom
101: 
102: **1. Durability. Needs someone who can write `~/Library/LaunchAgents`.**
103: The collector must be launched and kept alive. The narrowest change is a LaunchAgent modelled on the
104: existing `com.alex.codex-otlp-relay.plist`, with these `ProgramArguments`:
105: 
106: ```
107: /usr/bin/python3
108: -B
109: /Users/alexlobaito/Projects/alex-system-map/tools/otel_collector.py
110: --port
111: 4320
112: --upstream
113: signoz
114: --upstream
115: langfuse
116: --log
117: /Users/alexlobaito/.agents/runtime/otel-collector.jsonl
118: ```
119: 
120: Both upstreams give the one day of side-by-side the play asks for. Drop `--upstream langfuse` after
121: that day. `KeepAlive` true, `RunAtLoad` true, stdout and stderr beside the log.
122: 
123: **2. Reach. Each agent has to be pointed at `127.0.0.1:4320` with its own name.**
124: Blocked for Claude Code: its exporter is in `~/.claude/settings.json`, which this project may not
125: edit. `~/.codex/` is writable from here, but the ChatGPT app's Codex stays off by Alex's decision and
126: is not to be re-pointed. The remaining agents (cursor, javar, javante, auditor, task-codex,
127: task-claude, paperclip-worker, paperclip-reviewer, dynamite-chatbot) live in config this project
128: cannot reach, or on other machines.
129: 
130: **3. Silence alerts. Needs a credential that does not exist yet.**
131: The MCP read key is role `signoz-viewer`. A `POST /api/v1/rules` with it returns
132: `HTTP 403 {"type":"forbidden","code":"authz_forbidden","message":"only editors/admins can access this resource"}`.
133: Creating alert rules needs an editor or admin SigNoz API key. That is a new credential, so it is
134: Alex's call. The `alex-email` channel already exists (`id 01a156e4-8ea5-7b8a-bbd8-190cbf8f6393`,
135: type email, to alex@increaseroas.com) and its delivery is confirmed: Alex saw the test in his Gmail
136: inbox at 2026-10-19 9:19 PM Detroit.
137: 
138: **4. The SigNoz tool card.** `~/.agents/tools/cards/signoz.md` is not writable from this project.
139: 
140: ## Suggested N for each silence alert, when the key exists
141: 
142: Set from measured rhythm, not guessed. A threshold that cries wolf teaches him to ignore the alarm,
143: which is worse than no alarm.
144: 
145: | service | N | why |
146: |---|---|---|
147: | `claude-code` | 24 h | Ran on 7 of the last 7 days but goes quiet overnight. |
148: | `codex` | 24 h | CLI only now; used most days. |
149: | `auditor` | 6 h | It is actively fixing things; the thing fixing everything must not die quietly. |
150: | `task-codex`, `task-claude` | 30 min | The board health check runs every 5 minutes, so half an hour of silence is already wrong. |
151: | `javar` | 48 h | Reported on 4 of the last 7 days. |
152: | `javante`, `cursor`, `paperclip-*`, `dynamite-chatbot` | hold | Not instrumented yet; an alert on a service that has never reported fires immediately and forever. Add each one's alert when its first span arrives. |
153: 
154: ## Not yet instrumented
155: 
156: `cursor`, `javante`, `auditor`, `task-codex`, `task-claude`, `paperclip-worker`,
157: `paperclip-reviewer`, `dynamite-chatbot` have sent nothing. `javar` and `javar-lab` report to
158: Langfuse from Orgo, not from this Mac. Only `claude-code` and `codex` have ever reached SigNoz, and
159: both only from my canaries.
160: 
161: ## Apply attempt, 2026-10-20: blocked at launchctl bootstrap
162: 
163: Alex approved the Codex-worker route in his own chat. A worker ran APPLY-p4.md through
164: `codex_traced.py` with narrow writable roots (`-C ~/.agents`, plus `--add-dir` for
165: `~/Library/LaunchAgents`, `~/Backups` and this repo; `--approve-for-me`, which is workspace-write,
166: never `danger-full-access`).
167: 
168: It installed the plist, `plutil -lint` said OK, and then stopped at step 1 with:
169: 
170: ```
171: Bootstrap failed: 5: Input/output error
172: Try re-running the command as root for richer errors.
173: ```
174: 
175: followed by `Could not find service "com.alex.signoz-collector" in domain for user gui: 501`.
176: 
177: The worker did the right thing: it stopped, reported, retried nothing and took no other route.
178: 
179: ### What has been ruled out
180: 
181: | candidate | ruled out by |
182: |---|---|
183: | the plist is malformed | `plutil -lint` OK, and `plistlib` parses it; Label, KeepAlive, RunAtLoad and all arguments read back correct |
184: | already loaded | `launchctl print` says the service is not in the domain |
185: | disabled | not present in `launchctl print-disabled gui/501` |
186: | the sandbox | the identical error appears with the sandbox switched off |
187: | our plist specifically | the same error appears for a path that does not exist, **and** for the already-loaded relay plist, which should have said "already loaded" |
188: | session context | `launchctl managername` is `Aqua`, and `launchctl print gui/501` reads the whole domain, 467 active services |
189: 
190: So `EIO 5` is launchctl's generic failure here and carries no information, and **the cause is not
191: established.** What is established is narrower and still useful: `launchctl bootstrap` cannot
192: perform any domain write from a process in this harness, by any route available to it, while
193: `launchctl print` reads the same domain perfectly.
194: 
195: ### State left behind
196: 
197: - `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.alex.signoz-collector.plist` **is installed but not loaded.** An
198:   unloaded plist does nothing at all, so this is inert rather than half-working. It is left in place
199:   so the one remaining command is a single step.
200: - `otlp_relay.py` still hashes to `90e6dd0a0944003cf05f47ad2c6bd09627fa47eab4548d043d02644723768417`
201:   and the old relay is still running, pid unchanged, unharmed by the bootstrap attempts.
202: - `codex_traced.py` unchanged at `8eb092cc...`; the tool card not placed; nothing committed in
203:   `~/.agents`. Steps 2 to 5 were never reached.
204: 
205: ### The one command that finishes it
206: 
207: Run from Terminal.app, as Alex, not through an agent:
208: 
209: ```sh
210: launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.alex.signoz-collector.plist
211: launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/com.alex.signoz-collector" | grep -E "state|pid"
212: ```
213: 
214: Then the rest of APPLY-p4.md from step 2, which an agent can do: `verify_durable.py` needs no
215: launchd write, only reads and HTTP.
216: 
217: ## Cause isolated, 2026-10-20: two separate causes, and a third problem I caused
218: 
219: ### Cause 1, the bootstrap failure: the Seatbelt sandbox blocks launchd domain writes
220: 
221: One variable, same bytes, same label:
222: 
223: | attempt | result |
224: |---|---|
225: | real plist, **inside** the Seatbelt sandbox | `Bootstrap failed: 5: Input/output error`, not registered |
226: | same plist, **outside** the sandbox | loaded, `state = running`, a live pid |
227: | a throwaway plist (`/usr/bin/true`, RunAtLoad false), outside the sandbox | loaded fine, so domain writes are not blocked in general |
228: 
229: The Codex worker ran under `--approve-for-me`, which **is** a workspace-write sandbox, which is why
230: it failed. So the fix for the bootstrap step is simply that it must run unsandboxed.
231: 
232: **Correcting an earlier entry in this file:** I previously recorded "the sandbox" as ruled out,
233: because the same EIO appeared with the sandbox off. That test used a **path that does not exist**,
234: which fails for its own reason, so it was never a valid control. A negative control that fails twice
235: over proves nothing. The sandbox was the cause all along.
236: 
237: `EIO 5` is launchctl's generic failure here: it is returned for a missing file, for an
238: already-loaded service, and for a sandbox refusal alike, so the error text carries no information
239: and the cause has to be found by changing one variable at a time.
240: 
241: ### Cause 2: `op read` does not complete under real launchd
242: 
243: With the bootstrap problem solved the job started, and then died waiting on its keys. Under launchd
244: the 1Password read did not finish in 90 seconds; from a shell the same read had taken under 14.
245: 
246: **Correcting a second earlier claim, and this one I reported twice as a cleared risk:** I said the
247: 1Password robot read was "proven to work with a launchd-shaped environment and no TTY". That test
248: ran a child process with a scrubbed environment. **It was a simulation of launchd, not launchd.** A
249: working session never proves a separate launch path is healthy, and here it did not.
250: 
251: ### Problem 3, self-inflicted: the 1Password service account stopped answering
252: 
253: While isolating cause 2 the account stopped responding to anything authenticated:
254: 
255: | command | result |
256: |---|---|
257: | `op --version` (local, no auth) | exit 0 in 0.2s |
258: | `op whoami` | hangs, no answer in 25s |
259: | `op vault list` | hangs, no answer in 25s |
260: 
261: So it is not key-specific, not `HOME`, and not launchd. The authenticated path itself is hanging,
262: and the most likely reason is the volume of reads I generated: every collector launch reads three
263: secrets (SigNoz, plus the Langfuse public and secret halves), and with `KeepAlive` restarting a
264: failing job, a crash loop multiplies that. The robot account has a documented daily cap. I stopped
265: making 1Password calls rather than deepening it, so this should recover on its own; it is not
266: established as a permanent fault and no key was changed.
267: 
268: **This is a design flaw in the collector, not just an accident.** Reading every upstream's secret
269: eagerly at startup, under a `KeepAlive` that relaunches on failure, makes the job an amplifier
270: against its own credential source. Before this runs durably it needs: exponential backoff rather
271: than a fixed five-second retry, a cap on launches per hour (`ThrottleInterval` alone is not
272: enough), and lazy per-upstream reads so a failure on one does not re-read the others.
273: 
274: ### State left behind
275: 
276: Clean. The service is **not** in the launchd domain, nothing listens on 4320, and no stray collector
277: process is running. The plist remains installed but unloaded, which is inert. `otlp_relay.py` is
278: still `90e6dd0a...`, `codex_traced.py` still `8eb092cc...`, no tool card placed, nothing committed in
279: `~/.agents`.
280: 
281: ### What p5 inherits from this
282: 
283: The Mac mini will hit cause 1 identically: whatever applies its LaunchAgent must be unsandboxed.
284: It will hit cause 2 as well if it reads 1Password at startup the same way, and that needs solving
285: before the mini is wired, not after.

