13: ======================= CORRECTION, appended after the run =======================
14: WRITTEN AT: see below. The lines above are the original capture and are NOT edited.
15: 
16: The CONCLUSION line above is WRONG and contradicts the data in this same file. The
17: count is correct: OP_INVOCATIONS is 0, and there are zero invocation lines between the
18: "---- invocations ----" and "---- end invocations ----" markers.
19: 
20: The bug was mine, in the capture script, not in the collector. It read:
21:     n=$(grep -c "  op " "$OPLOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
22: `grep -c` prints "0" AND exits non-zero when it finds nothing, so the `|| echo 0` also
23: ran and n became the two-line string "0\n0". The test `[ "$n" = "0" ]` therefore failed
24: and the script printed the negative branch over a zero count.
25: 
26: WHAT THE DATA ACTUALLY ESTABLISHES: a PATH shim was placed ahead of the real binary for
27: the whole run and logged every invocation. It recorded none. A macOS "op would like to
28: access data from other apps" prompt can only be raised by an op process, so no prompt
29: was possible during this run, and both keys were served from the login Keychain cache.
30: 
31: The script has been fixed to count with `grep -c ... ; true` and to compare an integer.
32: This correction is appended rather than applied in place, because editing a captured
33: evidence file after the fact would make every other capture here worth less.
34: CORRECTION WRITTEN AT: 2026-10-20T03:55:09Z

